course: dictionary
lesson: 8340
title: Rome: a city, an empire, and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so context tells you which one you need. «The empire was split into a western half ruled from Rome and an eastern half ruled from Constantinople.» Here Rome is a place that ruled something, so it means the centre of a great empire, not just a city. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence?
explain: «Rome left us roads, laws, the alphabet, the names of months, languages, buildings and the Christian church.» This long list is a clue. It shows Rome was not just one place but something that changed the world for many years.
explain: Rome also appears in sayings. «When in Rome, do as the Romans do» means follow the customs of the place you are visiting. «Rome wasn't built in a day» means big things take a long time. A saying does not mean exactly what its words say, so the context matters.
fact: Rome is the capital city of Italy.
fact: Rome was the centre of the ancient Roman Empire, which ruled much of Europe more than two thousand years ago.
fact: Several smaller towns, for example in the USA, are also called Rome.
fact: Rome is a proper name, the name of a particular place, so it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Rome» can mean the city or the empire ruled from it, depending on the sentence.
exercise: «The empire was split into a western half ruled from Rome and an eastern half ruled from Constantinople.» In this sentence, what does Rome mean? | A kind of bread | *The centre of the Roman Empire, the place it was ruled from | A river in Italy | A small town in the USA
exercise: Which city is the capital of Italy? | Turin | Florence | Milan | *Rome
exercise: Why does «Rome» start with a capital letter? | Because it is the first word of every sentence | Because it appears in a list | Because it is a very long word | *Because it is the name of a particular place
test: «Rome left us roads, laws, the alphabet and the names of months.» Which of these is NOT in the list? | *Chocolate | The alphabet | Roads | Laws
test: Someone says, «When in Rome, do as the Romans do.» What do they mean? | Romans must do everything twice | *Follow the customs of the place you are visiting | Never go to Italy | Only eat Italian food
exam: «Several smaller towns, for example in the USA, are also called Rome.» If you read Rome in this sentence, what should you think? | *A small town that happens to share the name of the famous city | A city in Italy ruled by emperors | A hill with a legend about it | The name of a month
